<article class="env-example" id="050R">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/050R">Example <span data-tag="050R">3.2.2.13</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{G}$ be a groupoid and let $x$ be an object of $\mathcal{G}$, which we identify with a vertex of the Kan complex $X = \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathcal{G} )$ (see Proposition <a data-tag="0037" href="/tag/0037">1.3.5.2</a>). Then: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
The set $\pi _{0}(X) = \pi _{0}(X,x)$ can be identified with the collection of isomorphism classes of objects of $\mathcal{G}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The fundamental group $\pi _{1}(X,x)$ can be identified with the automorphism group $\operatorname{Aut}_{ \mathcal{G}}(x)$ of $x$ as an object of $\mathcal{G}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The homotopy groups $\pi _{n}(X,x)$ are trivial for $n \geq 2$, since an $n$-simplex $\sigma : \Delta ^ n \rightarrow X$ is determined by the restriction $\sigma |_{ \operatorname{\partial \Delta }^{n} }$ (see Exercise <a data-tag="050G" href="/tag/050G">1.3.1.5</a>). 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
